00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.800
One such sequel that premiered earlier this year was "How to Train Your Dragon".

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.280
Now, this is a franchise we've never covered here,

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.120
mostly because I'd never seen the movies up until recently.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.640
But having just caught up with the franchise after a few really long flights,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.680
I'm finally sinking my teeth into these tales of Toothless,

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.280
by addressing the biggest question that comes out of the whole theatrical empire:

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.180
Not "Could dragons exist?"

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.500
No, not "How do massive lizards fly?".

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.540
It is way more obvious than that!

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:51.160
Today, I am gonna answer the question of "How do you train your dragon?"

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.909
And the answer is... not like you see in the movie!

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:57.419
I mean, I know the movie makes it seem all magical and heartwarming,

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.200
but when you look at the realities of what's actually going on on screen,

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.449
the entire premise goes up in flames.

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:06.389
You should NOT train your dragon.

00:02:06.389 --> 00:02:07.540
I'm not talking about

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:11.660
"Oh, dragon training would be dangerous, because they'll eat you and they'll burn you with their hot fire!"

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.220
No, the entire concept of training your dragon is simply broken at a fundamental level,

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.500
and you definitely shouldn't do it the way that Hiccup does in the movies.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.280
So saddle up, Vikings of questionable geographic origin!

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.459
"Are you sure you want that kid running the village?"

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:28.220
It is time to theorize!

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.440
For anyone was like me and missed out on this franchise,

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.280
here's basically what you need to know for today's theory.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.200
Hiccup lives on the island of Berk with his father, the chief.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.340
Berk is prone to frequent dragon attacks.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.860
So, like most people being tormented by a species of wild animals, they fight back.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:48.860
In one of the dragon attacks, Hiccup injures an elusive Night Fury dragon that he eventually names Toothless, and befriends.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.580
Over the course of the film through his interactions with Toothless,

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:54.940
Hiccup learns that dragons are really just glorified cats,

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:58.760
that can be won over with beams of light, chin scratches and dragon-nip!

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:02.639
Children in Berk's society have to undergo a process known as "dragon training"

00:03:02.639 --> 00:03:04.639
"I've decided I don't wanna fight dragons."  "I think it's time you learned to fight dragons."

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:05.349
"You go first."

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:09.320
"You get your wish. Dragon training, you start in the morning."

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.100
"Oh man, I should have gone first."

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:17.020
And Hiccup skyrockets in popularity, as his tactics serve as a non-violent model to follow for the rest of the village.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.380
By the end of the film, everyone in the village has their own pet dragon,

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.080
completely forgetting that these are enormous deadly wild creatures,

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.100
who were literally at their throats only a few hours before.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.029
Sure, they were being mind-controlled by a giant dragon overlord,

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.970
but still, you've been fighting these things for generations and now you're like,

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.060
"Oh, look at how cute they are!"

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.480
No! Stupid! Bad idea!

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.040
And this episode's gonna tell you why.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:44.180
Now, let's make one thing clear: By the end of the movie, what we see aren't trained dragons, they're domesticated. Dragons.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.319
That may seem like a minor point, but it's actually a huge difference.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:53.140
You see in the real world, we use a lot of words about animals interchangeably that aren't really the same at all,

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:58.080
and when it comes to training a dragon, the difference between words like "train" and "domesticate"

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.220
is the difference between getting you the world's most awesome pet,

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.480
and you getting personally barbecued to death.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.740
So let's get our definitions straight.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:09.020
To tame an animal is to take an individual wild animal

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.060
and make that specific animal not afraid of you as a human.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:16.120
To train an animal is to get it to repeatedly follow a command.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.440
You blow a whistle, your dog sits.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.360
To domesticate an animal is the most intense of these three,

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:26.580
and requires fundamentally altering the species over many generations via selective breeding.

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:30.359
Basically transforming it to behave the way that humans want.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.780
So today we're gonna address the possibility of all three, but specifically focus on training.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:36.700
Since you know, that's the title of the movie.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.600
Now Hiccup, for all his supposed dragon knowledge,

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.320
is not the example of proper training that the people of Berk should be following.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.360
What he does is dangerous, destructive and worst of all, inhumane.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:53.200
Now that may sound extreme, until you take a second look at the events of this film,

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.280
without all the sappy feel-good music.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.500
What happens to kick off this entire movie?

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:03.220
Hiccup injures Toothless, wrecking his tail flap, an injury that completely grounds Tootless.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:05.980
I meant Toothless not Tootless.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.200
Not a dragon without farts.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.780
So we are starting the relationship with a critical injury, and then it proceeds to get even worse.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.760
Hiccup moves on to reconstruct a new tail piece for Toothless,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.420
and while it's all real nice and good that he works so gosh darn hard on it,

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:21.869
it was repairing an injury that was his own fault.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:25.100
And as soon as that tail piece is on, guess what Toothless does?

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.700
Does he turn around and thank Hiccup?

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.300
Does he tearfully tell him that he'll miss their companionship?

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:30.239
No, he flies away!

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.040
He tries to escape the human predator!

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.890
Like a wild animal should and would do in that moment.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.480
But even then, Hiccup can't leave well enough alone,

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:38.980
because he hangs on through the air,

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:45.840
and discovers super conveniently that the dragon can only fly if he - Master Human is controlling the prosthetic tail piece.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:47.540
Just think about that for a second.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:52.580
This would be like clipping a bird's wings and replacing its wing tips with remote-control wing tips,

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:54.220
so we could fly them around like a model airplane.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:59.672
When you really think about what's going on here, to a wild animal who's scared and injured,

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.240
it would be immediately classified as animal cruelty,

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.860
or at the very least, a pretty barbaric way to get a big animal to do what you want.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.530
And Hiccup sees no problem here.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.480
In fact, he spends most of his time riding Toothless around, congratulating himself about how amazing he is.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:16.260
"Yes!"

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:17.470
"Yes, I did it!"

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:20.860
After that, he goes on to chase down the dragon to get a saddle on it,

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:24.760
again not voluntary since Toothless will literally die if he can't fly.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:28.720
Then he has to integrate all kinds of reins and turning systems to control the dragon,

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.660
instead of rigging a system where, you know, Toothless could control it himself.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:36.120
Hiccup doesn't design or even attempt to design,

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.660
something that would allow Toothless to fly independently,

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:41.240
until the short film "Gift of the Night Fury".

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:45.180
And even then he still has systems in place that allow him to maintain control.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:46.584
It's like saying to your dog,

00:06:46.584 --> 00:06:49.880
"I love you, as long as I still have the ability to shock collar you!"

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:54.240
It's only in the third and final film that Hiccup finally respects this animal enough,

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.400
that he designs something that makes him completely independent.

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:04.619
Meanwhile, every time Hiccup falls, Toothless crashes headfirst into the ground at full speed, or directly into cliff faces.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.440
Even when his control system works, and Hiccup is managing to navigate, think about what's happening here.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:11.980
Sure, it looks epic, and they're working like a team,

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:15.720
but in reality, this dragon is a helpless passenger in his own body.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:19.200
He is a slave and no matter how many times it happens,

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:26.200
he only has one choice but to keep going, because again, a dragon that can't fly is a dead dragon.

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:32.240
The long story short here is that whether he had good intentions or no, Hiccup used force to train his dragon.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.939
He stripped away its independence and Toothless was required to submit to him.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:38.880
So what should Hiccup have done to train his dragon?

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.980
Well, it comes as no great surprise to anyone that it would, at the very least, be a lot harder

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.320
to train a gigantic lizard than it looks like here.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.880
But is there any world where this is possible?

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:51.560
Using anything even remotely like the real methods of animal training,

00:07:51.569 --> 00:07:53.480
could you train your dragon?

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.760
Maybe there's a way that this story could have turned out better.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:00.400
Maybe we call it: "Film Theory's Guide To How To Actually Train Your Dragon".

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.740
It's... catchy...?

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.160
Maybe it just needs an acronym - F.T.G.H.T.A.T.Y.D.!

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:05.820
*Crowd cheers*

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.860
So let's look at what Hiccup should have done if he were actually training a dragon,

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:12.200
or any kind of reptile that even remotely resembles a dragon,

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.200
because I want to give him a fighting chance here.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:16.680
Referring to reputable zoo and reptile resource guides,

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:22.560
the F.T.G.H.T.A.T.Y.D. immediately encounters a few issues, other than its terrible name.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.820
Because in most places you look that respond to the question of "How do I train my reptile?",

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.340
they answer with "Ehhhhh... you don't."

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:34.300
To quote Safer Pets, an online resource about training and caring for exotic animals:

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:39.540
"First it's important for you to realize that your pet lizard will never be like a dog - or even a cat."

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:44.400
"You will never be able to have the same level of interactions and trick training as with a pet dog."

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.080
"If this is what you're after, then you might be better to consider a different sort of pet."

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:51.620
Maybe not with that attitude, Safer Pets!

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:54.500
No, but seriously, why are they so negative towards this whole thing?

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:58.320
Is it just that we haven't spent the last few millennia domesticating our local crocodiles,

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.150
and that's why they're not willing to play fetch?

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:01.700
Well, no.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:07.520
It turns out that the problem with training your dragon, or any other reptile, starts with the brain.

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:10.410
Have you ever heard anyone refer to their "lizard brain"?

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:13.640
You usually use the phrase when you're referring to some impulse decision,

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.780
or reaction that you just can't control,

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:18.880
like your involuntary reaction to a jump-scare in a horror movie,

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:23.720
or how I seem to eat the entire box of Springtime Oreos before I even realize it.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.180
Every single spring!

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:27.200
They're the basic Oreos, but I still am like,

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:29.360
"Ah, they're yellow frosted! Great!"

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.420
These are all primal reactions.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:33.280
It tastes good, so eat it.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.160
It's scary, so run away.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:40.460
These "lizard brain" actions are controlled by the three most primal areas of our brain:

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:43.420
The brain stem; the cerebellum, and the basal ganglia,

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:48.999
which aptly enough, are the same areas of the brain we share with lizards, including reptiles.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.200
Reptile brains are just much smaller than mammal brains relative to body size,

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:55.840
because they only need to have these three areas.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:03.780
For reference, a saltwater crocodile and a horse have almost identical weight ranges, from 800 to 2200 pounds.

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:08.460
A crocodile brain weighs in at about 8.4 to 15 grams,

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:14.820
while a horse's brain weighs in at 530-655 grams.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.678
So why are their brains so much bigger?

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.260
Well, humans and other mammals, like dogs and cats,

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:24.340
have other areas of the brain layered on top of those primal areas called the limbic system,

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.760
which allow us to have more complex emotions,

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:31.620
like how dogs experience excitement to see their owners, or depression when they're abandoned.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:37.040
The limbic system also enables us to develop trust and behavioural pattern recognition,

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:39.980
that allows us to train and be trained.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:45.180
Reptiles literally don't have the parts of the brain necessary to develop relationships with humans.

00:10:45.189 --> 00:10:51.538
Their brains are missing the emotional cognition to be trained to do the types of tricks that you see in "How to Train Your Dragon".

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:52.739
"No, come on!"

00:10:52.739 --> 00:10:53.720
I hear you saying,

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:57.640
"There's videos on YouTube of people training lizards, like Komodo dragons, duh!"

00:10:57.640 --> 00:10:59.369
"They even have "dragon" in their name!"

00:10:59.399 --> 00:11:04.500
But that kind of lizard training looks very different than training a cat and a dog.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:06.958
The most advanced dragon trainers in the world,

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:09.680
who work with big lizards like Komodo dragons,

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:13.280
can only train them to do what's known as "target practice".

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:17.100
Lifting a rifle onto their shoulder and shooting at a target downrange.

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:18.418
I just did that to see if you were paying attention.

00:11:18.489 --> 00:11:21.080
No, you cannot train your lizard to shoot a gun.

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:24.980
Target practice here means basically, classical conditioning for your dinner.

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:28.620
Instead of throwing some mice, or other animals into the reptiles habitat,

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:30.140
trainers take a target,

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:33.160
basically a ball or a plate on a stick into the enclosure,

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:36.880
and train the reptile to touch the target in order to get it's food.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:37.680
That's it.

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:41.620
Like that is the level of training that you can do for your dragon.

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:42.520
The end.

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:46.060
Biologically speaking, it is impossible to do much more than that,

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:52.740
and the reason trainers typically even bother doing this is to help move reptiles to different areas of their enclosure,

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:55.480
to receive things like medical care, or give them exercise.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:58.715
Not become helpful pets, or even show off for the zoo visitors.

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:02.160
Reptiles in captivity are known for being lazy and lethargic,

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.980
because they don't have to defend their territory anymore.

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:08.020
Their lizard brain is no longer needed for survival,

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:13.180
so the only way to get them appropriate activity is to get them to work for their food.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:19.839
Even the crocodile hunter himself, Steve Irwin, describes using himself as bait in crocodile enclosures,

00:12:19.839 --> 00:12:25.479
so the crocs would regain their sense of territorialism and fend him off to get their blood pumping.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:29.920
This is a very rudimentary form of training, and it certainly isn't for fun.

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:33.820
It's because to keep the reptiles healthy, they have to use their instincts,

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:37.280
because that's the only thinking that they're capable of doing.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:41.180
Anything you train a reptile to do isn't them being loyal to you,

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:44.120
it's just them using an instinct.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:46.940
All that said, for all you reptilian apologists out there,

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:51.020
it's important to understand the difference between intelligence and emotional complexity.

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:52.960
Reptiles are extremely smart,

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:57.640
so just because they're not emotionally capable doesn't mean they're not capable-capable.

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:00.480
They have memories that span almost their whole lifetime,

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:06.420
and big reptiles like alligators can navigate 30 - 40 miles of waterways easily from when they're born.

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:10.220
Which can be said for basically none of us who are working on or watching this video,

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:12.760
because human babies are virtually useless.

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:15.700
Reptiles are also pretty much the kings of Darwinism,

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:22.000
where a lot of female reptiles will only give the green light, so to speak, to the biggest strongest male in their area,

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.770
meaning that the only genes that get passed down are from the very best of their species.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:31.080
But we as humans who don't really care about that, and instead just want a cuddly iguana,

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:38.160
can't mistake all of that for love or loyalty, or trust, or any of the emotions that we hope to get out of a real pet.

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:41.340
Reptiles still aren't domesticated after all these millennia.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:44.220
But that means that whether you're adopting a Dragon from the wild,

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:46.180
or you're picking up a Chameleon at the pet store,

00:13:46.279 --> 00:13:48.840
(Literally the pet that I've wanted all throughout elementary school),

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:52.088
you are adopting the equivalent of a wild animal.

00:13:52.089 --> 00:13:54.360
So how do you train your dragon?

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:59.180
1.You set realistic expectations that you won't ever have a true relationship with them,

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:02.380
2. You beat them around an enclosure to give them lots of exercise,

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:07.990
and 3. You appreciate that they're instinctually way smarter than us, but don't know how to brag about it.

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:11.049
Relationships just don't figure into that equation.

00:14:11.049 --> 00:14:15.240
Which is a real bummer, because it would be totally amazing to ride your alligator in Florida,

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:17.960
or watch your basilisk lizard skip across your bathtub.

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:20.900
But at the end of the day, you cannot train your dragon,

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:24.549
which means that the F.T.G.H.T.A.T.Y.D. is finished.

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:27.800
And that means back to the drawing board for me.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:28.569
The dragon circus...

00:14:28.569 --> 00:14:29.620
No.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:34.520
Kangaroo best friend- a kangaroo can be your best friend!

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:38.980
Yes, and away!

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:40.140
But before I go, remember!

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:43.760
That is just a theory- a Film Theory!

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:45.310
Aaaaand cut!

